  "worked_solution": "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\u222c}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={\u222c}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\u2202z}{\u2202x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\u2202z}{\u2202y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=x+{y}^{3},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066535/assets/87845c65dba220978ca81bd5a2b27816ca15f951cd14d8fe499ab43d3c64ef77.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202z}{\u2202x} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(x+{y}^{3})=1, \\\\ \\frac{\u2202z}{\u2202y} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(x+{y}^{3})=3{y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={\u222c}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\u2202z}{\u2202x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\u2202z}{\u2202y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\u222c}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(1)^{2}+{(3{y}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\u222c}_{D}\\sqrt{2+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\sqrt{2+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
